{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"best-stealthy-lorikeet","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# A shell-expanded comment body wrote live credentials into the ticket store; only GitHub push protection caught it\n\n## What happened\n\nAn agent posted a ticket comment whose body quoted an upstream error message containing\nbacktick-delimited text. The body was passed to `rebar comment` inside a DOUBLE-QUOTED shell\nargument, so zsh performed COMMAND SUBSTITUTION on the backticks. The backticked token happened\nto be `local`, a zsh builtin, which with no arguments prints every shell and environment\nvariable. That dump was interpolated into the comment body and committed to the tickets store.\n\nThe resulting COMMENT event was 17,821 characters where the intended text was a few thousand,\nand contained live secrets read straight out of the environment:\n\n* an Anthropic API key (`sk-ant-`, 108 chars)\n* TWO GitHub personal access tokens (`ghp_`, 40 chars each)\n* an OpenAI-style service key (`sk-svc-`, 167 chars)\n* a Google-style API key and several base64 blobs\n\n## Blast radius — nothing reached a remote, and the reason matters\n\nGitHub secret-scanning PUSH PROTECTION rejected the push:\n\"push declined due to repository rule violations ... To push, remove secret from commit(s)\".\nVerified afterwards: the offending commit is on NO remote branch and is NOT an ancestor of\norigin/tickets. Every unpushed commit was scanned and exactly ONE commit / ONE file is affected.\n\nSo the credentials never left the machine. **The only control that stopped this was a\nthird-party scanner on the far side of the push.** rebar itself accepted the body, signed it,\ncommitted it, and would have pushed it to a mirror of a real repository. That is the finding:\nthere is no rebar-side control at all, and the one that saved us is outside the project.\n\nA second consequence worth noting: because push protection blocks the whole ref update, the\npoisoned commit wedged 108 unrelated commits of legitimate ticket history behind it. A single\nbad body therefore also becomes an availability problem for the store's durability.\n\n## Why this is rebar's problem and not merely operator error\n\nThe proximate cause is a quoting mistake by the caller, and callers will keep making it: agents\nroutinely paste provider error text, log lines and code snippets into comments, and that text\nfrequently contains backticks. The store's job is to persist what it is given, but it currently\npersists a credential dump silently and irreversibly (event files are append-only and signed),\nand the damage is only discoverable at push time, far from the cause.\n\nNote also the asymmetry: rebar ALREADY understands that secrets must not be logged in some\nplaces, and the review-bot's own token exposure is tracked separately. There is no equivalent\nguard on the one surface that writes attacker-useful text to a git history.\n\n## Scope\n\n- the comment/description write path (whatever validates an event body before it is committed\n  and signed) — a pre-write secret screen.\n- optionally the CLI argument surface, so a body arriving with a shell-expanded variable dump is\n  recognisable.\n- documentation for agents on passing untrusted text to `rebar comment` safely.\n\n## Approach\n\nTwo independent layers, and the first is the one that matters:\n\n1. **Screen event bodies for high-confidence secret shapes before committing** — the same\n   prefix families GitHub's scanner uses are cheap, low-false-positive anchors (an\n   Anthropic/OpenAI key prefix, a GitHub token prefix, an AWS access-key id). On a hit, REFUSE\n   the write with a clear error naming the matched family and offset. Refusing is correct rather\n   than redacting: a body containing a live credential is almost never the body the author meant\n   to write, so silently truncating it would hide the mistake that produced it.\n2. **Make the shell hazard hard to hit** — document the safe invocation (write the body to a\n   file and pass it via a form the shell does not re-scan) and prefer a stdin/file-based body\n   argument over a positional string for long bodies.\n\nDeliberately NOT proposed: scanning the whole store retroactively, or weakening the append-only\nevent model. The guard belongs at the write boundary.\n\n## Testing\n\n- a body containing a synthetic token of each screened family is REFUSED, with the family named,\n  and NO event file is written and NO commit is created. Verify RED by removing the screen.\n- a body containing look-alike-but-harmless text (a hex string, the literal word 'sk-ant' with no\n  key material, a base64 blob under the length floor) is ACCEPTED, so the screen does not become\n  a nuisance that agents learn to work around.\n- a body legitimately quoting backticked text is stored VERBATIM when passed by the documented\n  safe path, proving the fix does not merely forbid the content that triggered the incident.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] an event body containing a high-confidence secret shape is refused at the write boundary,\n      before any event file is written, signed, or committed — proven by a test verified RED.\n- [ ] the refusal names the matched secret family and does not echo the secret value.\n- [ ] look-alike benign text is not refused, evidenced by explicit negative cases, so the screen\n      does not train agents to route around it.\n- [ ] the safe way to pass untrusted or quoted text as a comment body is documented where agents\n      will read it, with the failure mode named (shell command substitution on backticks).\n- [ ] the incident is recorded with its blast radius, including that the only control which\n      stopped it was outside the project.","id":"e4f4-295b-2c03-44c4","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Ticket store persists shell-expanded credential dumps; only GitHub push protection caught a live PAT leak"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785817837755593001,"uuid":"c226ac83-bacc-4009-b126-6e6692e87406"}